  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render, and the rule hasn't been through a run yet.\n\n**Data:** The sample logs have one document per web request. The fields I used are `timestamp`, `response` (a string, so `response.keyword` is the usable form), `bytes` and `extension`. The data also has geo, client IP, host, user agent and OS fields.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-6d0f8fce Web logs overview\" has four panels and a default range of the last 7 days. It is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-6d0f8fce-web-logs-overview-e6d1335a1471.\n- Total requests (single number)\n- Requests over time (line)\n- Requests by response code (pie)\n- Average bytes by file extension (bar)\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-6d0f8fce 5xx spike\" (id `ac21b1cf-9fd2-4ac7-a433-688ed564f61b`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- It checks every minute.\n- It looks at the last 5 minutes for documents where `response.keyword` matches `5xx`, and fires when there are more than 10.\n- Rules are created disabled by default, so I enabled it to make it actually check.\n- It has no action attached, so when it fires it will show up in Kibana's alerts but won't send a notification anywhere.",
